<title>BELGIUM: EU proposes new money for debt relief.</title>
<headline>EU proposes new money for debt relief.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-03-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission proposed on Wednesday setting aside 100 million ecus ($115 million) over four years to help relieve the debts of poor African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) countries.</p>
<p>The money would benefit 11 countries that are classified under an international action plan as heavily indebted poor countries (HIPC) - Burundi, Ethiopia, Guinea-Bissau, Madagascar, Mozambique, Niger, Rwanda, Sao Tome-Principe, Zaire, Zambia and Uganda.</p>
<p>The HIPC Debt Initiative, endorsed last year by the International Monetary Fund and World Bank, aims to ensure that development programmes of poor countries are not hindered by excessive debt burdens.</p>
<p>&quot;The European (Union), as a major development partner of these countries, has an important role to play in this initiative,&quot; Development Commissioner Joao de Deus Pinheiro said in a statement.</p>
<p>The Commission proposed setting aside 25 million Ecus ($29 million) a year over the period 1997-2000 to provide structural and debt-management assistance and to reduce the net value of the ACP countries' debt to the EU. That debt amounts to about 300 million Ecus ($345 million).</p>
<p>The plan must be approved by ministers from the 15 EU countries. -- Brussels Newsroom 32 2 287 6800</p>
